          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been anxious for months and it is starting to interfere with daily life. How is anxiety assessed, what does guidance describe as first-line treatment, and where does it sit in ICD-10-CM classification?",
          "answer": "If you have thoughts of harming yourself, or feel unable to keep yourself safe, get urgent help right away. The urgency check found no emergency pattern and rated your situation routine. That does not rule out a serious cause, so seek medical advice promptly if your symptoms become severe, get worse, or include anything new and unexplained. Chest pain with breathlessness, sudden breathlessness, or collapse also needs urgent assessment, because a heart or lung cause must be ruled out first (*Generalized anxiety disorder*).\n\n**How it is assessed.** Guidance describes persistent, excessive worry about a range of everyday matters, occurring more days than not for at least six months, hard to control, and causing impairment. Your pattern matches that description, but only a clinician can assess it (*Generalized anxiety disorder*). A seven-item questionnaire, the GAD-7, is commonly used to screen and track severity. Scores of 5, 10 and 15 mark mild, moderate and severe, and a score of 10 or more usually prompts further assessment. A score is not a diagnosis (*Generalized anxiety disorder: screening instruments and first-line care*). A fuller assessment also rules out physical causes such as thyroid disease and heart rhythm problems, reviews caffeine, alcohol and medicines, and checks for depression, which often occurs alongside anxiety.\n\nSeveral of your symptoms, such as tiredness, poor sleep, headache, and palpitations, can also occur in other conditions. The retrieved material mentions insomnia, migraine, iron-deficiency anemia and common cold as candidates the tool flagged. I cannot tell you which, if any, applies to you.\n\n**First-line treatment.** Guidance lists two options of broadly similar effectiveness, chosen together with the person (*Generalized anxiety disorder: screening instruments and first-line care*):\n- **Cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT)**, given individually, in a group, or through a guided digital program. It has the largest evidence base among the talking therapies.\n- **Medication from the SSRI or SNRI classes.** Whether either is suitable for you is a decision for a clinician. The retrieved guidance names these classes but gives no further detail on specific drugs.\n\nRegular physical activity, relaxation and mindfulness are described as useful additions, not replacements (*Physical activity and anxiety*; *Generalized anxiety disorder*). The retrieved passages do not give a formal evidence grade for either option, so I can't say how strong the evidence is beyond \"largest base\" for CBT.\n\n**Where it sits in ICD-10-CM.** Anxiety disorders fall in Chapter 5, mental, behavioral and neurodevelopmental disorders (F01–F99). The anxiety group is F40–F48, which includes phobias, panic, generalized anxiety disorder, obsessive-compulsive disorder, and stress-related conditions such as post-traumatic stress disorder (*ICD-10-CM Chapter 5*). I could not retrieve detail on how a clinician picks a specific code within that range.\n\nAnxiety lasting months and affecting daily life is a good reason to see a clinician, who can carry out this assessment and talk through the treatment options with you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
